@@ -0,0 +1,83 @@
+# Gaudi2 block-FP8 preparation
+
+The Gaudi2 development path currently provides a standalone C++ host preparation
+tool and exact numerical qualification. The separate
+[native resource adapter](gaudi2-runtime.md) supplies SynapseAI device/Host storage,
+streams and DMA fences, with offline lifecycle qualification. It does not execute
+a model on HPU. The ordinary NInfer Engine and CUDA build keep their existing path.
+The [static BF16 GEMM Op](gaudi2-bf16-gemm.md) supplies the first native recipe
+and launch ownership path, with real-SDK compile and offline graph/math checks.
+Physical TPC/MME execution remains unverified; GDN, full-KV generation and KVMem
+device integration remain to be implemented.
+
+Build without CUDA or the Gaudi SDK (CMake 3.28+, C++20, Python 3.11+):
+
+```sh
+cmake -S backends/ninfer -B build-gaudi2-host -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
+  -DNINFER_GAUDI2_PREPARATION_ONLY=ON
+cmake --build build-gaudi2-host --config Release -j
+ctest --test-dir build-gaudi2-host -C Release --output-on-failure
+```
+
+Run these commands from the wrapper repository after preparing its pinned NInfer
+integration. For a standalone prepared NInfer tree, replace `-S backends/ninfer`
+with `-S .`. With Visual Studio, the executable is under
+`build-gaudi2-host/src/core/gaudi2/Release/`; a single-configuration generator
+places it in `build-gaudi2-host/src/core/gaudi2/`. The generated
+`gaudi2-tile-executable-Release.txt` records the actual executable path.
+
+```sh
+ninfer-gaudi2-fp8-tile weights.bin scales.f32 17408 5120 128 256 128 128 tile.bf16
+```
+
+The positional arguments are codes file, scales file, matrix rows, matrix columns,
+first tile row, first tile column, tile rows, tile columns and output file.
+Optional `--codes-offset BYTES` and `--scales-offset BYTES` select the absolute
+data-plane offsets inside checkpoint files. Both files may be the same
+safetensors shard. The wrapper's `scripts/gaudi2/inspect_fp8_checkpoint.py`
+validates safetensors metadata and emits compatible commands; the C++ plane
+reader itself does not parse a safetensors header. Without an explicit offset,
+the corresponding raw file must contain exactly its declared matrix plane.
+
+The numerical profile is
+`hf-e4m3fn-block128-f32-to-bf16-rne-w8a16`:
+
+- Codes are row-major HF `F8_E4M3` / E4M3FN, including finite values up to 448.
+- Scales are little-endian FP32, row-major
+  `[ceil(N/128), ceil(K/128)]`. HF `weight_scale_inv` values are dequantization
+  multipliers, not reciprocals. BF16 scale planes are not accepted by this tool.
+- Every tile element is decoded and multiplied in FP32, then rounded to BF16
+  using round-to-nearest, ties-to-even. Output is compact row-major BF16 words,
+  little-endian. Negative zero and finite subnormals are preserved at their
+  defined cast boundaries. Nonfinite codes/scales, negative scales and values
+  that overflow BF16 are rejected before output is opened.
+- This is W8A16 preparation for a future bounded TPC-dequant/BF16-MME route.
+  Gaudi2 native FP8 has a different numerical range. No direct byte
+  reinterpretation, native FP8-MME execution or W8A8 quality claim is made.
+
+Only the selected code rows and intersecting block scales are read. The tool
+allocates `3 * tile_rows * tile_columns + 4 * intersected_scale_count` payload
+bytes: one code tile, one BF16 output tile and one FP32 scale tile. This number
+excludes stream/allocator/process overhead and the operating system's file cache.
+It is reported as `host_tile_buffer_bytes` on stdout. The default hard payload
+limit is 64 MiB; use `--max-host-tile-bytes BYTES` to explicitly choose another
+limit. Range and budget checks occur before allocation/read. A 128x128 tile
+aligned to block boundaries consumes 49,156 payload bytes independently of the
+full matrix size. It does not materialize a whole BF16 model.
+
+The core decoder takes caller-owned compact tile planes. Inputs must be disjoint
+from output; overlap is rejected. Invalid numerical input leaves output untouched.
+The CLI rejects overwriting either input file, including filesystem aliases.
+An output I/O failure may leave a partial output file; this is a preparation
+utility, not an atomic artifact publisher.
+
+Tests compare all 256 FP8 codewords to an independent FP64 mathematical formula,
+exercise BF16 ties, subnormals, overflow and invalid-input output preservation,
+and compare file tiles across unaligned 128-block boundaries with a Python
+nearest-representable-BF16 oracle. Real Qwen FFN geometry checks bounded storage;
+it does not replace real-checkpoint/HPU numerical and memory qualification.
+
+The layout follows the explicit block-FP8 contract of
+[1Cat's dequantization/BF16 path](https://github.com/1CatAI/1Cat-vLLM-Gaudi/blob/e2266e0efa381e6649e0f25b789cbb61b21b0e1f/flashinfer_gaudi/block_scaled.py)
+and the existing NInfer HF block-FP8 source decoder. No external kernel source is
+copied into this host implementation.
